A by-election was held in the [[Tāmaki Makaurau (New Zealand electorate)|Tāmaki Makaurau electorate]] on 6 September 2025. The by-election was triggered by the death of [[Takutai Tarsh Kemp]], an MP for [[Te Pāti Māori]].

Broadcaster [[Oriini Kaipara]] held the seat for [[Te Pāti Māori]], defeating [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] MP [[Peeni Henare]], who previously held it between 2014 and 2023.

== Background ==
=== Constituency ===
The electorate covers central and southern [[Auckland]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Council |first=Auckland |title=Tāmaki Makaurau / Auckland's heritage |url=http://www.aucklandcouncil.govt.nz/arts-culture-heritage/heritage/Pages/tamaki-makaurau-aucklands-heritage.aspx |access-date=9 July 2025 |publisher=[[Auckland Council]] |language=en}}</ref> At the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 election]], [[Takutai Tarsh Kemp]] of [[Te Pāti Māori]] defeated the incumbent [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] MP [[Peeni Henare]] by a margin of just 42 votes.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Sowman-Lund |first=Stewart |date=26 June 2025 |title=Tāmaki Makaurau by-election: What happens next? |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/360736985/tamaki-makaurau-election-what-happens-next |website=[[The Post (Wellington)|The Post]]}}</ref>

=== Trigger ===
The by-election was triggered by the unexpected death of the sitting MP, Kemp, on 26 June 2025.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{cite news |date=26 June 2025 |title=Te Pāti Māori MP Takutai Tarsh Kemp dies |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360736208/te-pati-maori-mp-takutai-tarsh-kemp-dies |access-date=26 June 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]}}</ref>

The process for calling a by-election when an MP dies is that once the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages receives the registration of death for a sitting MP, they must inform the [[Speaker of the New Zealand House of Representatives|speaker of the House of Representatives]] within 12 hours.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM308558.html |title= Section 58: Registrar of Births and Deaths to notify Speaker of death of member – Electoral Act 1993 No 87 (as of 1 July 2012) – New Zealand Legislation |publisher= [[Parliamentary Counsel Office (New Zealand)|Parliamentary Counsel Office]] |access-date= 9 May 2013}}</ref> The speaker of the House must then publish, without delay, the notice of vacancy of the seat in the ''[[New Zealand Gazette]]''.<ref name="s129">{{cite web |url= http://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309454.html |title= Section 129: By-elections for members representing electoral districts – Electoral Act 1993 No 87 (as of 1 July 2012) – New Zealand Legislation |publisher= [[Parliamentary Counsel Office (New Zealand)|Parliamentary Counsel Office]]  |access-date= 9 May 2013}}</ref> The notice was published in the ''Gazette'' on 9 July 2025.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://gazette.govt.nz/notice/id/2025-ps3738 |title=Notice of vacancy in seat in House of Representatives |date=9 July 2025 |work=[[New Zealand Gazette]] |access-date=9 July 2025}}</ref>

The [[Governor-General of New Zealand|governor-general]] must issue the [[writ of election|writ]] for a by-election to fill a vacant electorate seat within 21 days of the ''Gazette'' notice (i.e. 30 July 2025).<ref name="s129" /> Because polling day must be a Saturday<ref name="s139">{{cite web |title=Section 139: Contents of writ – Electoral Act 1993 No 87 (as of 1 July 2012) – New Zealand Legislation |url=https://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309482.html |access-date=9 July 2025 |publisher=[[Parliamentary Counsel Office (New Zealand)|Parliamentary Counsel Office]]}}</ref> and two weeks are generally required for the counting of special votes, the last possible polling day was Saturday, 13 September 2025. 

On 14 July, the prime minister, [[Christopher Luxon]], announced that the by-election would be held on Saturday, 6 September.<ref name="rnz">{{cite web |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/566861/tamaki-makaurau-by-election-to-be-held-in-september |title=Tāmaki Makaurau by-election to be held in September |date=14 July 2025 |work=[[RNZ National]] |first=Tuwhenuaroa |last=Natanahira |access-date=14 July 2025}}</ref> The writ must be returned with the successful candidate within 60 days of its issue,<ref name="s139" /> which meant the writ had to be returned by 28 September 2025.

== Candidates ==
In early July, former journalist [[Oriini Kaipara]] announced her intention to seek the nomination to be Te Pāti Māori's candidate for the by-election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |date=7 July 2025 |title=Former broadcaster Oriini Kaipara puts name forward for Te Pāti Māori's Tāmaki Makaurau candidate |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/566190/former-broadcaster-oriini-kaipara-puts-name-forward-for-te-pati-maori-s-tamaki-makaurau-candidate |access-date=7 July 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz}}</ref> Youth worker Te Kou o Rehua Panapa also declared intention to seek the Te Pāti Māori nomination, while [[Eru Kapa-Kingi]], a list candidate in 2023, ruled himself out.<ref>{{cite web |last=Natanahira |first=Tuwhenuaroa |date=10 July 2025 |title=Te Pāti Māori meeting tonight to decide candidate for upcoming Tāmaki Makarau by-election |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/566463/te-pati-maori-meeting-tonight-to-decide-candidate-for-upcoming-tamaki-makarau-by-election |access-date=10 July 2025 |work=[[RNZ]]}}</ref> Party members of Te Pāti Māori voted for their candidate,<ref>{{Cite news |last= |date=9 July 2025 |title=Journalist Oriini Kaipara seeking Te Pāti Māori candidacy for Tāmaki Makaurau seat |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/national/former-broadcaster-oriini-kaipara-seeking-te-p%C4%81ti-m%C4%81ori-candidacy-for-t%C4%81maki-makaurau-seat/ |access-date=9 July 2025 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |language=en-nz}}</ref> with the selection taking place on 10 July and the successful candidate formally announced the next day.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Tāmaki Electorate Candidate Selection Update |date=9 July 2025 |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA2507/S00067/tamaki-electorate-candidate-selection-update.htm |access-date=10 July 2025 |author=[[Te Pāti Māori]] |agency=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]]}}</ref> Kaipara was confirmed as the nominee on 10 July.<ref>{{cite web |date=10 July 2025 |title=Oriini Kaipara will represent Te Pāti Māori in Tāmaki Makaurau by-election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360753860/oriini-kaipara-will-represent-te-pati-maori-tamaki-makaurau-election |access-date=10 July 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]}}</ref>

Previous Tāmaki Makaurau MP, [[Peeni Henare]], told reporters on 26 June that he had not yet thought about whether he would stand in the by-election, saying "that's not where my mind is".<ref name=":0" /> On 13 July, [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] confirmed Henare as their candidate.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |title=Labour confirms Peeni Henare to contest Tāmaki Makaurau by-election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360755837/labour-confirms-peeni-henare-contest-tamaki-makaurau-election |access-date=13 July 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=13 July 2025}}</ref>

[[New Zealand National Party|National]], [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]], [[New Zealand First]] and the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Greens]] all announced they would not run candidates.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Milne |first=Jonathan |date=11 July 2025 |title=Tāmaki Makaurau by-election throws up curious quirk of MMP |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2025/07/11/tamaki-makaurau-by-election-throws-up-curious-quirk-of-mmp/ |url-access=subscription |access-date=11 July 2025 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]]}}</ref><ref name="nzh" /> [[Vision NZ]] leader [[Hannah Tamaki]] contested the seat again, having received 829 votes in 2023.<ref name="nzh">{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/tamaki-makaurau-byelection-labour-set-to-choose-candidate-peeni-henare-emerging-as-favourite/OPA6TYN5EFFFVCMJWGEFHGV3FY/ |title=Tāmaki Makaurau byelection: Labour set to choose candidate, Peeni Henare emerging as favourite |date=11 July 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Adam |last=Pearse}}</ref>
