== Campaign ==
A candidate debate was held at Ngā Whare Waatea Marae in [[Māngere]] on 20 August hosted by [[Radio Waatea]]. Henare and Kaipara were the only candidates invited to participate. [[Karl Mokaraka]], a former Vision NZ candidate, disrupted the event by protesting the exclusion of Vision NZ’s candidate, Hannah Tamaki. Mokaraka was escorted out amid audience heckles as Kaipara led a ''waiata'' to calm the crowd. The remaining debate proceeded cordially with candidates discussing issues like housing, the cost of living, education, Māori issues and foreign affairs.<ref name="stuff_360798234">{{cite web |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360798234/watch-tamaki-makaurau-election-debate-hit-heckler |first=Sam |last=Smith |title=Tāmaki Makaurau by-election debate hit by heckler |date=21 August 2025 |access-date=21 August 2025 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref> During that same debate, both Henare and Kaipara voiced support for repealing the [[Gangs Act 2024]]'s ban on [[gang patch]]es. Henare's remarks contradicted the Labour Party's official position of not repealing the gang patch ban; with both Labour leader [[Chris Hipkins]] and deputy leader [[Carmel Sepuloni]] issuing statements that Henare's remarks did not represent official Labour policy. Te Pāti Māori co-leader [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] said that her party spoke for Māori while Labour represented mainstream policy. National Party MP and [[Minister of Justice (New Zealand)|Justice Minister]] [[Paul Goldsmith (politician)|Paul Goldsmith]] questioned Labour's commitment to not repealing the gang patch ban.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour candidate Peeni Henare stands by gang-patch law repeal claim |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/570694/labour-candidate-peeni-henare-stands-by-gang-patch-law-repeal-claim |access-date=24 August 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=22 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250824031104/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/570694/labour-candidate-peeni-henare-stands-by-gang-patch-law-repeal-claim |archive-date=24 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 4 September 2025, Te Pāti Māori MP [[Tākuta Ferris]] published a controversial social media post criticising the ethnicity of several Labour Party campaign volunteers during the 2025 Tāmaki Makaurau by-election, accusing them of trying to steal a Māori seat. In response, Te Pāti Māori disavowed Ferris' post, issued an apology and ordered him to remove it. Labour MP [[Willie Jackson (politician)|Willie Jackson]] condemned Ferris' social media remarks as offensive and reiterated his party's commitment to representing all ethnic communities.<ref>{{cite news |title=Te Pāti Māori apologies over Tākuta Ferris'social media post|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/572068/te-pati-maori-apologises-over-takuta-ferris-social-media-post |access-date=5 September 2025|work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=4 September 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ruru|first1=Karanama |last2=Burr |first2=Lloyd |title=Te Pāti Māori for MP's 'totally unacceptable' social post |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360813304/he-should-know-better-labour-condemn-te-pati-maori-mps-social-media-post |access-date=5 September 2025|work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=4 September 2025}}</ref>

==Results==
Oriini Kaipara won the election with 6,948 votes – a margin of 3,519 votes over Peeni Henare, who received 3,429 votes. Hannah Tamaki received 175 votes, Sherry-Lee Matene received 41, and Kelvyn Alp received 26.<ref>{{cite web |title=Tāmaki Makaurau - Official Result |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/2025_tamaki_makaurau_byelection/ |website=electionresults.govt.nz |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=18 September 2025}}</ref><ref name="Stuff 6 Sep 25">{{cite news |last1=Williams |first1=Caroline |last2=Burr |first2=Lloyd |title=‘Looking forward to fighting a good fight’: Oriini Kaipara wins Tāmaki Makaurau by-election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360815477/tamaki-makaurau-election-polling-closed-candidates-wait-results |access-date=6 September 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=6 September 2025 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250906095422/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360815477/tamaki-makaurau-election-polling-closed-candidates-wait-results |archive-date=6 September 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="1News 7 Sep 2025">{{cite news|title=Oriini Kaipara decisively wins Tāmaki Makaurau by-election|url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/09/07/oriini-kaipara-handily-wins-tamaki-makaurau-by-election/ |work=[[1News]] |date=7 September 2025|access-date=6 September 2025|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250908004732/https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/09/07/oriini-kaipara-decisively-wins-tamaki-makaurau-by-election/|archive-date=8 September 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Henare conceded defeat on the night of the by-election while Kaipara gave an acceptance speech and song to her supporters.<ref name="RNZ 6 Sep 2025">{{cite news|title=Labour's Peeni Henare conceded Tāmaki Makaurau by-election to Te Pāti Māori rival Oriini Kaipara|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/572310/labour-s-peeni-henare-concedes-tamaki-makaurau-by-election-to-te-pati-maori-rival-oriini-kaipara |work=[[RNZ]] |date=6 September 2025|access-date=7 September 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250906111821/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/572310/labour-s-peeni-henare-concedes-tamaki-makaurau-by-election-to-te-pati-maori-rival-oriini-kaipara |archive-date=6 September 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> 

Turnout was low, with only around 27.1% of the 44,269 people enrolled to vote in the Tāmaki Makaurau electorate taking part.<ref name="1News 7 Sep 2025"/>
{{2025 Tāmaki Makaurau by-election}}

==Post-election events==
On 6 September 2025, the Electoral Commission confirmed it would investigate a social media post released on the last polling day by Te Pāti Māori MP Tākuta Ferris that urged people to vote for his party.<ref name="1News 7 Sep 2025" /><ref name="RNZ 6 Sep 2025" /> The Commission also confirmed they were investigating two billboards and a gazebo left standing by the party.<ref name="RNZ 6 Sep 2025" />

